332639/field-2/2d6cfdfd554881720879744154242fb5aa6f8fe5d2ee6c2ca18d39657060f0e9" :answer-field/choices ["q-332639/field-2/d17d0a0aed00c915829ac638c636d58c85e58020e48b1bb4d70c111bcb120c8c" "q-332639/field-2/07c71ea73bb8110bc7a6da95b843ea949731374fc7b33be3dc0291a2b916365d" "q-332639/field-2/2d6cfdfd554881720879744154242fb5aa6f8fe5d2ee6c2ca18d39657060f0e9" "q-332639/field-2/fc20968d883ca769dfb3874d84601b4e956d52d67b276c788d49f3a972f23c95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-332639" :question/id #uuid "4465eca2-fa42-514f-bf86-62fcd4c733a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-332639" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Find equations of the $x$- and $y$-nullclines for the corresponding phase space.\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-nullcline is $y=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- The $x$-nullcline is a line for which ${x}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are vertical). So, to find it, we set the first equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\n- The $y$-nullcline is a line for which ${y}^{′}=0$ (i.e., the tangents to trajectories are horizontal). So, to find it, we set the second equation of the system equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n-3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=3x\n$$\n\nPlotting these in the phase space, we obtain the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068646/assets/b6eb04363e97559b51ae43439d965eb19fd7f6acca57916f3159317ceba033ba.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-332639/field-1" "q-332639/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d219270-5202-54e6-8cba-dc816155bd7b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-332639"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-332658/field-1/e819017e19dcc